        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 848,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 546,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Counts the values in a tensor: the product of its shape.\n\nA 3 by 4 tensor holds 12 values, a 2 by 3 by 4 one holds 24. Use it to check that a reshape can work before trying it (the new shape must multiply to the same number), to size a buffer to match, or to scale a sum into an average.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.numel\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.numel\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor. Every tensor that arrives is counted. A list or NumPy array is turned into a tensor first, so t.numel counts a list's numbers too. An empty tensor or an empty list gives 0.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nnumel out:\nThe number of values, as an int.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nA metro makes a fresh t.rand tensor three times a second. Pick a shape from the menu: the heat_map shows the tensor (rows by columns) so you can count the cells, t.info shows the shape, and t.numel the count - 12 for 3x4, 48 for 6x8, 16 for 1x16. 2x3x4 has three dimensions, which the heat_map cannot draw as one picture, but t.numel still answers 24.\n\nRELATED:\nt.info gives the shape itself. t.reshape and t.view need a new shape with the same count. np.shape gives a NumPy array's shape.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 530,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Counts the values in a tensor: the product of its shape.\n\nA 3 by 4 tensor holds 12 values, a 2 by 3 by 4 one holds 24. Use it to check that a reshape can work before trying it (the new shape must multiply to the same number), to size a buffer to match, or to scale a sum into an average.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.numel\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.numel\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor. Every tensor that arrives is counted. A list or NumPy array is turned into a tensor first, so t.numel counts a list's numbers too. An empty tensor or an empty list gives 0.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nnumel out:\nThe number of values, as an int.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nA metro makes a fresh t.rand tensor three times a second. Pick a shape from the menu: the heat_map shows the tensor (rows by columns) so you can count the cells, t.info shows the shape, and t.numel the count - 12 for 3x4, 48 for 6x8, 16 for 1x16. 2x3x4 has three dimensions, which the heat_map cannot draw as one picture, but t.numel still answers 24.\n\nRELATED:\nt.info gives the shape itself. t.reshape and t.view need a new shape with the same count. np.shape gives a NumPy array's shape."
        },
